{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Colorado Rockies entered the season with modest expectations and briefly justified cautious optimism with a strong 12-9 April. C.J. Cron emerged as the offensive centerpiece early, launching a three-run blast on April 19th that signaled he would be the club's most dangerous bat all year. But the bullpen — particularly closer Daniel Bard — was already showing cracks, surrendering walk-off and late-inning blows that would become a recurring theme throughout the summer.\n\nMay and June told the story of a team unable to sustain momentum. Despite bright spots from Jose Iglesias, Brendan Rodgers, and Ryan McMahon, the Rockies went a combined 21-34 over those two months. Bard and the relief corps repeatedly gave away leads in the late innings, and the offense, reliant on the thin air of Coors Field, could not manufacture enough runs away from home to stay competitive. The 5.06 ERA was damning, and no stretch of clutch hitting could fully paper over a rotation and bullpen in chronic distress.\n\nJuly offered a fleeting reprieve. The Rockies went 13-14, nearly playing .500 ball, fueled by Cron's power surge — his 19th homer on July 3rd a three-run missile that typified what he meant to the lineup. But August brought the season's cruelest stretch, a 10-18 collapse bookended by bullpen implosions and late-game heartbreaks. Alex Colomé surrendered a devastating two-run single on August 5th, and even bright offensive moments from Cron, Grichuk, Elias Díaz, and Charlie Blackmon could not overcome the structural flaws of this roster.\n\nSeptember and October arrived without any playoff stakes, and the Rockies limped to a 12-19 finish. Young relievers Justin Lawrence and Gavin Hollowell absorbed brutal late-inning lessons, including a stunning three-run homer by Thairo Estrada that served as one of the season's cruelest final blows. Colorado finished 68-94, outscored by 175 runs, a team that showed enough offensive life to entertain at Coors but never possessed the pitching to genuinely contend. The 2022 season was ultimately a holding pattern — flashes of what Cron, McMahon, and Blackmon could provide, surrounded by a pitching staff that simply could not keep them in enough games.",
